:assert26a a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q26a ;
  cito:extends :assert23 ;
  dcterms:description "P1/P3/P4 confirmed under the app's defaults; P2 refuted; the decision rule has no bundle-free answer" ;
  schema:text "The grid-connectivity bias survives the move from synthetic rings to real ride endpoints: under the app's default physics the 8-direction optimum sits 11.90% above the sq128 near-continuum reference (5.99/2.38/0.73% at 16/32/64), monotone on 86/86 pairs, with no lattice direction signature once corpus imbalance is removed (12.35 pp spread → 3.35 pp). But sq16 recovers only 49.69% of the gap [CI 45.9, 53.8], so the pre-registered ≥50% decision rule FAILS by 0.31 pp — while its CI straddles the threshold and the per-pair estimator reads 51.57%, which would pass." ;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy :run26a ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e26_grid.csv .

:assert26b a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q26a ;
  cito:extends :assert26a , :assert21 ;
  dcterms:description "the ladder verdict is a function of the rider's cost bundle, not of the grid alone" ;
  schema:text "The grid bias scales with climb-dominance β/(aRoll+aAero), measuring what Entry 25 §7 asserted but never swept: halving the ratio (53.5:1 → 28.5:1 by swapping the app defaults for an Entry-20 calibrated rider set) nearly halves the sq8 gap (11.90% → 6.82%) and lifts sq16's recovery to 57.72%. Each declared bundle therefore CONFIRMS the prediction the other REFUTES — P1 holds on bundle 1 and fails low on bundle 2, P2 the reverse — and the pre-registered 'ship 16 directions?' rule flips from FAIL to PASS. A second, corpus-independent qualification: the gap shrinks with route length (15.79% on the shortest third of pairs vs 10.25% on the longest), which explains the apparent per-rider spread." ;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy :run26a ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e26_grid_cal.csv .

:assert26c a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q26b ;
  dcterms:description "P5 confirmed on 9/10 strata, P6 confirmed, P7 refuted (junction-step artifact)" ;
  schema:text "Portal-correcting the PROFILE moves measured-energy accuracy and bias in the right direction wherever viaducts exist: v2Edge@igc5 med|Δ%| 22.13 → 18.32 on the urban censo rides, 14.79 → 13.21 on the author's corpus, 10.24 → 9.35 pooled over 922 rides, with |bias| shrinking on 9 of 10 corpus × model strata (the exception is JAAM under the champion R0, whose already-negative bias moves 0.18 pp further negative)." ;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy :run26b ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e26_portal_profiles.csv .

:assert26c_ev1 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:disputes :plan26 ;
  cito:supports :assert26c ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e26_portal_profiles.csv ;
  schema:text "P7 REFUTED with a mechanism: h₊ drops on 697/915 touched rides (76.2%), not ≥90% — censo 96.4% (median 20.1 m removed) but JAAM only 49.2% (median −0.1 m, ascent sometimes ADDED), and the ascent-rose group has shorter spans and less relief removed (1079 m / 2.3 m vs 1415 m / 16.3 m). Cause: junction steps where a deck endpoint sampled at the OSM abutment meets the 5 m profile grid. The artifact belongs to transplanting the portal model into a PROFILE; the deployed portal is a single Dijkstra edge with no junctions." .

:assert26d a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q26b ;
  dcterms:description "P8 and P10 confirmed; P9 not evaluated; reach is the sensitive endpoint" ;
  schema:text "In the discretized scenario portals leave the median optimal route identically unchanged (ΔE median 0.000 kJ — most optimal paths never need a bridge) with a large tail (down to −7.07% at 8 directions, −8.95% at 16; 33% of pairs improved), and the gains are broadly nDirs-independent. The sensitive endpoint is AREA: portals expand budget-reachable area on 79 of 86 pairs (+0.49% median at 8 directions, +0.64% at 16), so for accessibility KPIs portals matter more as an area correction than a per-route one. Portal benefit is itself bundle-dependent (33% → 21% of pairs improved under the calibrated set), for the same β reason as the ladder." ;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy :run26a ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e26_grid.csv .

:assert26e a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q26a ;
  dcterms:description "secondary endpoint, uninformative as specified; interpretable only on genuine journeys" ;
  schema:text "The pre-registered detour ratio E_opt/E_trackwalk is swamped by a corpus artifact: after the 800 m endpoint floor the surviving pairs are mostly near-loops (median route detour 12.4×, P. Paz 24.8×), so the pooled ratio of 0.070 measures 'this ride was not a journey'. Restricted to genuine journeys (track ≤ 2× straight line; 21 rides over 12 pairs) it reads 0.467 at sq8 and 0.421 at sq128 — on a real A→B journey roughly half the ride's energy is street-constraint and detour rather than terrain necessity. Related corpus fact: 595 of 922 rides (65%) are loops and are dropped by the floor." ;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy :run26a ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e26_detour.csv .

# ── Entry 27 — re-baseline to São Paulo gravity ──────────────────────────────

:q27 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "What moves when the model's gravity is re-baselined from the textbook 9.81 to São Paulo's measured local value (9.7864, IAG-USP)?" ;
  dcterms:source :journal .

:run27 a prov:Activity ;
  prov:used :compare.py , :censo_compare.py , :eps_hypothesis.py , :eps_sp_test.py ,
            :ppaz_compare.py , :jaam_compare.py , :danlessa_compare.py , :time_compare.py ,
            :param_fit.py , :cda_estimate.py , :regime_compare.py , :igc_resolution_test.py ,
            :goal_calibration.py , :scale_trio.py , :bootstrap_ci.py ;
  rdfs:comment "Constants-only change: G swept to 9.7864 across all 14 rider-physics sites, flat_eq_speed reverted from a Cardano closed form to the applet's bisection (numpy dropped — analysis/ is stdlib-only again), V8-exactness retired as an invariant. No model, harness logic or corpus changed, so every moved number moves for exactly one reason." .

:assert27 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q27 ;
  dcterms:description "nine medians move by ≤0.2 pp in the predicted direction; every rider-transfer result is unchanged" ;
  schema:text "A 0.24% reduction in gravity moves the assumed-parameter corpora and leaves the fitted ones alone. Longões: champion 3.6 → 3.5, canonical 5.1 → 5.2, off 19.3 → 19.1 med|Δ%|; censo: canonical 6.5 → 6.6, smooth ε=0.15 5.0 → 4.8, while the poor-man's ε=0.20 headline holds at 3.9. Every rider-corpus result is unchanged to four figures (P. Paz 4.9/+0.6 and the frozen-ε 0.091-vs-0.139 win; JAAM 3.5/+0.4; the author 6.1), as is the ε closed form (37% RMS skill, correlations 0.30/0.60/0.77/0.82, part–whole 0.72/0.99) and the time model's endpoint (T1b 6.6 vs T0 7.6). Direction as predicted: less gravity charges less for climbing, so over-predicting variants improve and the under-predicting canonical worsens. Conservation residual unchanged at 1.77e-8; cross-language parity unchanged at 8514 comparisons ≤ 1e-9." ;
  prov:wasGeneratedBy :run27 ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :model_comparison.csv , :censo_comparison.csv .

:assert27_ev1 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:supports :assert27 ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :ppaz_comparison.csv ;
  schema:text "The mechanism behind the split: where mass is INVERTED from the data it absorbs the constant, so accuracy is gravity-insensitive — implied masses rise by exactly the predicted 1/G ratio (P. Paz 74.3 → 74.5 kg, JAAM 101.7 → 101.9, the author 74.5 → 74.7). Where mass is ASSUMED (the longões' sheet parameters, the censo's generic 78 kg) there is no free parameter, so the shift lands in med|Δ%| instead. A fitted corpus is insensitive to the constant; an assumed corpus is not." .

:assert27_ev2 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:supports :assert22 ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :model_comparison.csv ;
  schema:text "The re-baseline STRENGTHENS Entry 22's parity finding: paired, the champion is now closer than the canonical simulation on 24 of 44 rides (55%, sign test p = 0.65), down from 25/44 (57%, p = 0.45) — even less separable, so the article's 'parity, not beats' wording is now conservative rather than borderline. The other four paired tests are identical." .

:assert27_ev3 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:disputes :assert10 ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :eps_sp.csv ;
  schema:text "One claim weakens: the frozen rural offset ε_coast − 0.13 now scores RMS 0.09 against the in-sample flat ε = 0.20's 0.08 (both were 0.08), so 'it ties the flat constant selected in-sample on this very set' becomes 'nearly ties'. Entry 10's negative result is untouched — every braking predictor still has R² ≤ 0.14 and the mechanistic correction is still worse than a constant at 0.19." .

:assert27_ev4 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:supports :assert19 ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :igc_resolution_test.csv ;
  schema:text "Entry 19 survives re-baselining: censo (assumed mass) moves 22.1 → 21.9 vs 12.3 → 12.1 (gap 9.44 → 9.41 pp); the pooled rider corpora (inverted mass) are unchanged (9.6 vs 7.1, gap 3.64) — mass inversion absorbs gravity exactly; both decision thresholds stay triggered." .

:assert27_ev5 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:qualifies :assert27_ev1 ;
  schema:text "Correction: the first DEM-chain re-runs froze pre-re-baseline implied masses in code (regime_compare PHYS, time_compare PPAZ_MASS); with masses corrected to 74.5/101.9/74.7 the fitted corpora return to their published values (trio 0.9375/0.0632 exact; goal primary endpoint at 3.69/2.79/4.92) — the pre-registered absorption confirmed cleanly; the briefly-published lumpy-grid-search explanation is retracted." .

:assert26f a schema:Claim ;
  dcterms:type "methodological" ;
  dcterms:description "process lessons and disclosed deviations" ;
  schema:text "Three process findings. (1) PRIVACY: the first implementation pulled OSM spans per-pair using crop bboxes derived from ride endpoints — which invert back to those endpoints, often homes — contradicting the pre-registration's whole-raster pull; an adversarial harness review caught it mid-run, the 43 affected pairs were discarded, and the pull now covers a fixed 0.1° tile grid over the whole DEM (verified a strict superset of the per-pair pulls, so no coverage was lost). (2) The 'extra edges can only help' assert had to be relaxed from exact ΔE ≤ 0 to f32-aware (the engine stores E in f32, so portals reorder relaxation; worst residual 1.9e-6 kJ, rel 7.7e-8) and now reports its worst residual instead of implying zero. (3) Entry 25 §12's lesson 'pre-register distributions, not point ranges' needs a companion: pre-register the ESTIMATOR, since the decision rule's verdict changed with an equally-valid reading of 'recovers ≥50% of the median gap'." ;
  dcterms:source :journal .

# ── Entry 28 — one implementation: harness dedup + repo restructure ─────────

:q28 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Can the harness code's duplication (the canonical engine implemented 7x, the FIT parser in 7 drifted variants, six helper functions copied 5-13x each) be collapsed into one shared implementation without changing any published number?" .

:assert28 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q28 ;
  dcterms:type "methodological" ;
  schema:text "Yes: every duplicated function was proven bit-identical (or a strict superset) against real ride data before deletion, then the fast harnesses and the full bootstrap_ci gate battery were re-run and matched. Net effect: one Python implementation of the engines/parsers (src/bicycling_energy_model/), the retired V8-exactness machinery deleted (v8math.py, ~460 lines), and the JS parity harness (analysis/parity/) retired to git history since its job — proving the Python port equals the JS it replaced — is done. The repo was also restructured (src/, research/journal/, data/inputs|results/) for cohesion; zero behavioural change." ;
  dcterms:source :journal .

