  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `python3 src/harness/perride_invert.py` (INVERT_SMOKE writes a .SMOKE file)" .
:skc_comparison.csv a prov:Entity ;
  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `python3 src/harness/skc_compare.py` (D6; tracks start at riders' homes)" .
:e44_scurve_fits.csv a prov:Entity ;
  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `python3 src/harness/e44_scurve.py`" .
:e45_ridelevel.paper.csv a prov:Entity ;
  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `E45_TARGET=paper python3 src/harness/e45_ridelevel.py`" .
:e46_switch.csv a prov:Entity ;
  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `python3 src/harness/e46_switch.py` (second-order: reads e47_formselect.csv)" .
:e47_formselect.csv a prov:Entity ;
  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `python3 src/harness/e47_formselect.py`" .
:e48_equiv.csv a prov:Entity ;
  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `python3 src/harness/e48_equiv.py` (grain: comparison, not ride)" .
:e49_affine.csv a prov:Entity ;
  rdfs:comment "gitignored; `python3 src/harness/e49_affine.py`" .

:q29 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "How much of the published accuracy is an artefact of the assumed CdA, C_rr and ρ, rather than of the model?" .
:assert29 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q29 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "A pre-registered CdA × C_rr × ρ sweep on D2–D5 maps the envelope: the medians move at the several-point scale and BOTH engines move together, so parameter error is a shared-input effect rather than a differential advantage for either. ρ and CdA enter only as the product ρ·CdA, an exact degeneracy — so the sweep identifies a product, never the factors." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :param_sweep.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .

:q31 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "D1's published numbers came from condition-informed per-ride parameters. What happens under the frozen protocol every other corpus uses?" .
:assert31 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q31 ;
  schema:text "The calibration corpus re-run blind lands at 8.2% (F3) against the simulation's 8.4%, versus 3.5%/5.2% informed — an informed→blind shift of +4.6 pp on F3 and +3.2 on the simulation. Parity survives the protocol change; the absolute level does not. The same re-run discovered that 58 of D2's 62 rides are already in D5, which is why D2 is excluded from the headline pool." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :longoes_frozen.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .

:q33 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Can each ride's own telemetry replace the assumed constants — and does the law survive honest per-ride physics?" .
:assert33 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q33 ;
  schema:text "Mass, C_rr and CdA inverted from each ride's own segments give 3.9% pooled median error against the simulation's 4.0% — parity again, on 1,409 rides. This is partially in-sample per ride by construction and is labelled so: it answers 'can telemetry replace judgment?', not the frozen-transfer question." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :perride_invert.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .

:q43 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Does the frozen law survive a corpus sharing no rider, country, terrain, device or model-selection history?" .
:assert43 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q43 ;
  schema:text "Yes, and it reaches its closest parity there: on D6's 740 clean rides from four European riders, F3 with ε_d reads 3.16% against the simulation's 3.15%. The same corpus breaks F4's scalar c while leaving the form intact — the sharpest available evidence that c belongs to the elevation source rather than to cycling. Its riders pedal descents far more than the ultra-distance specialists, confirmed physics-free from descent occupancy, which does not embarrass the deficit but explains it." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :skc_comparison.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .

:q44 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Is the coasting deficit generated by a grade-conditional pedalling probability, and does that probability separate riders?" .
:assert44 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q44 ;
  schema:text "Descent pedalling occupancy fits a sigmoid in grade whose midpoint s50 separates the rider populations disjointly — ultra-distance specialists stop pedalling on gentler ground than seasoned amateurs. An initial 9-of-9 result was withdrawn: it scored the constant outside its own ≥3% scope and leaked the flat intensity from the ride being predicted. Rescored honestly, 7 of 9." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e44_scurve_fits.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .

:q45 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Should the deficit be a constant, or a function of the route's descent geometry?" .
:assert45 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q45 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "Out of sample on 513 held-out rides, the grade-inverse δ = k/s̄ with k = 0.0051 beats the frozen constant, winning 344/513 (p < 1e-4) and cutting held-out deficit error 0.067 → 0.055. Its domain is real descents only: k/s̄ exceeds 1 below s̄ ≈ 0.51%, so the form is defined for s̄ ≥ 3%. A companion flat-terrain probe was retracted mid-entry — it had selected segments on RAW ascent whose target rate sat below the corpus noise floor, making the search self-reinforcing; rebuilt on deadband-smoothed cells." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e45_ridelevel.paper.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .

:q46 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "§3.3 recommends the dynamic estimator only above a 3% mean descent grade, and no harness enforces it. What happens when the switch is built?" .
:assert46 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q46 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  dcterms:description "the rule is right; the frozen grid's sub-threshold cells are a cancellation" ;
  schema:text "The two parameter classes reverse the verdict: under frozen priors switching WORSENS the pooled median (5.08 → 5.62), under per-ride inverted physics it IMPROVES it (5.51 → 4.12). The bias column adjudicates — below 3%, ε_d is near-unbiased under frozen priors (+0.28) and badly biased under honest physics (−4.64), while ε_f = 0.20 is the mirror image (+5.90, +0.11). Below the threshold the coasting limit clamps, so the applied ε_d has median 0.544 against ε_f's 0.20, and that refund cancels the over-prediction of the frozen CdA = 0.40 (well above the 0.26–0.32 these riders invert to). So the rule is correct and the frozen grid's sub-threshold ε_d cells are accurate BY CANCELLATION rather than by fit." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e46_switch.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .
:assert46_ev1 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:disputes :assert45 ;
  schema:text "Registered prediction P3 — that unswitched, the grade-inverse form is unusable — FAILS. Unswitched it is better overall than the constant (4.83 vs 5.08), and worse only inside the sub-3% band by 0.12 pp rather than the anticipated 20–70%. That anticipated gap was a DEFICIT-space under-prediction; carried into energy it nearly vanishes." .

:q47 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Which deficit form should the paper ship, selected the way it should have been — on the calibration corpora only?" .
:assert47 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q47 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "Two selections on D1 ∪ D2 (48 rides at s̄ ≥ 3%), by BIC under a Laplace likelihood, under frozen priors and again under per-ride inverted physics: BOTH return the incumbent ε₀ = 0.13, with zero fitted parameters. The same contest on D3–D6 selects the grade-inverse form decisively (ΔBIC 128.8), so the disagreement is statistical power, not direction — 48 calibration rides cannot resolve what 990 evaluation rides can, and only the calibration arm is licensed to build the tables. Nothing published moved." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e47_formselect.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .
:assert47_ev1 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:supports :assert47 ;
  schema:text "Fitting the deficit against ENERGY does not recover it. Deficit-space refits return c = 0.134 and k = 0.0052 against the published 0.13 and 0.0051, from an independent implementation; energy-space refits return 0.044 and 0.0020, two to three times lower — a free δ buys down the law's positive bias instead of measuring descent pedalling. The physical reading of δ depends on keeping the fit in deficit space." .
:assert47_ev2 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:qualifies :assert47 ;
  schema:text "ΔBIC's scale is not the reader's scale: on D3–D6 the grade-inverse form beats the constant by ΔBIC = 128.8 and by 0.17 pp of median error, and in one arm it wins on BIC while being WORSE on the median. BIC scores the mean absolute residual; every statistic this project publishes is a median. Under AIC (flat 2k penalty) the champion does not change, but the P_a,g margin is ΔAIC = 1.9 against the 2.0 tie threshold — the incumbent survives that arm by a tenth of a unit." .

:q48 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "The parity claims rest on non-significant paired tests, which are an absence of evidence. Can they be upgraded to formal equivalence?" .
:assert48 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q48 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "TOST at a registered ±1.0 pp margin, 90% CI of the difference of medians under paired stratified resampling: four equivalent, six inconclusive, none outside the margin. Both transfer pools are formally equivalent — D3+D4 [−0.90, −0.33] and D3–D5 [−0.55, −0.07] — upgrading the headline claim from a failure to reject. D1 is inconclusive at n = 44 on both protocols, as predicted. Five of six inconclusive verdicts fail on the side where the LAW BEATS the simulation (D3's CI is wholly negative), so reading 'inconclusive' as a weakness of the law is backwards." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e48_equiv.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .
:assert48_ev1 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:disputes :assert48 ;
  schema:text "The exception cost the paper a sentence: D2 · F3's interval [−0.04, +2.18] allows the closed form being up to 2.2 pp WORSE than the simulation on urban stop-go riding, with a point estimate (+1.08) already outside the margin. That sentence had claimed parity on a non-significant sign test; it now says what the data support." .

:q49 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Does the coasting limit itself need rescaling — δ = k1·ε_coast + k2 — and is that rescaling a rider property?" .
:assert49 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q49 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "No on both counts. k1 > 0 is solid (0.92, 95% CI [0.46, 2.73] excludes zero, so the limit does over-credit), but the two-parameter fit is UNIDENTIFIED: the derived slope 1−k1 has CI [−1.73, +0.54], spanning inverted to substantially positive, because k1 and k2 trade off. The form is also dominated by the flat constant it nests at k1 = 1 — that has the lower BIC with half the parameters and identical held-out error — so the second parameter buys nothing. The per-rider arm (14 parameters) wins in sample and on within-rider held-out, but its fits disagree on SIGN across riders, one stays pinned to a bound, and it is the only form that pushes ε outside [0,1]. Frozen ε₀ = 0.13, keeping the geometry at full weight, has the best held-out error and bias of every form with fewer than 14 parameters." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e49_affine.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .
:assert49_ev1 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:supports :assert49 ;
  schema:text "The one identified number worth keeping: on real descents under honest per-ride physics the best FLAT ε is 0.344 [0.292, 0.394] — an interval excluding the published ε_f = 0.20. No contradiction, since ε_f was selected on D2's urban stop-go riding, a different regime; but it says the flat constant is regime-specific and 0.20 should not be carried onto real descents as universal." .

:q73 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "What does discretisation onto a routing lattice cost the published models — per direction count, terrain chain and portal treatment — and can a Dijkstra-deployable edge cost close the gap?" .
:assert73 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q73 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "The direction-count penalty is chain-shaped: on the smoothed 5 m survey it is essentially all path-length inflation (len_ratio 1.2737 at n = 4, 1.0543 at n = 8), on raw FABDEM essentially all noise (the lattice path reads 1.417x the polyline's h+ at n = 8). eF4 — the per-edge noise deduction at each chain's MEASURED c(tau=2) pin — beats eF2 on all six DEM chains and is the best deployable cost on FABDEM (41.62 vs 48.23 med|d%|). The deadband keeps a unique share at edge grain: F3 on the raw chain beats eF2 on the sigma-30-treated map on both populations (5.67 vs 9.21; 7.01 vs 11.49) — the filter can move into the map, but not all of it. The flat-vs-geometric epsilon contest is chain-dependent: grade-local wins on smoothed chains, flat on raw FABDEM. The portal deck helps 860/883 span-touched rides at sigma 10. Two populations all-arms-complete (IGC 1,034; FABDEM 1,844); every fitted constant read from its producing CSV; c pins measured from geometry only, worst disagreement with Entry 71's rates 0.14 m/km." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e73_gridpath.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .
:assert73_ev1 a schema:Claim ;
  cito:qualifies :assert73 ;
  schema:text "The quantizer's parity gates forced two fixes in the new harness (n = 1 positions from the raw geo track; full-precision sample coordinates) and exposed one in the published instrument: Entry 41's 30 m arms are evaluated after a 5 m re-gridding that clips extrema between nodes — median 3.7 m/ride (0.85% of h+) on the local survey, 15.5 m (1.48%) on FABDEM — one-signed, internally consistent within that letter, disclosed as its erratum." .

:q74 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Which descent-epsilon policy belongs in the deployed edge cost for Sao Paulo routing — flat (published or regional), grade-local geometric, grade-inverse deficit, or the occupancy mechanism curve?" .
:assert74 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q74 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "The incumbent holds, decisively: the deployed grade-local eps_geo wins every treated-map cell on both DTMs and both regions (3.31 med|d%| at the Sao Paulo sigma-30 n = 16 cell vs 5.11 for the published flat eps-2 and 10.24 for the regionally honest eps_SP = 0.2385 — the lower regional constant loses ~5 pp because the treated-chain bias is already positive). Entry 45's grade-inverse deficit trails by ~3 pp (its k/s term under-credits the shallow band treated chains live in) and the occupancy mechanism curve does not transfer from route cells to edges. On RAW chains the ordering inverts and flat eps-2 wins: noise manufactures steep micro-descents that grade-local policies refuse to credit — adaptive recovery and untreated noise do not mix. eps = 0 prices the descent term as worst everywhere. Five policies, identical edge skeleton, all constants frozen from producing CSVs; no candidate fitted on this experiment's energies." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e73_gridpath.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .

:q75 a schema:Question ;
  schema:text "Can the deadband's amplitude selectivity be moved into the MAP — 2-D prominence-tau (h-extrema) filtering of the raster — and beat the sigma-30 Gaussian the default uses?" .
:assert75 a schema:Claim ;
  schema:about :q75 ; dcterms:type "pre-registered" ;
  schema:text "No — refuted on both grids. At the primary cell (eps_geo, n = 16) the prominence-6 chain reads 7.43 med|d%| on the IGC 30 m lattice and 22.80 on FABDEM against the Gaussian's 4.23 / 4.96, with the paired test decisively against (41/150 and 16/140). The c pins carry the mechanism: prominence removes phantom ascent at 5.91 to 3.63 m/km (IGC) and barely 5.73 to 5.20 (FABDEM) vs the Gaussian's 2.53 / 2.99 — DEM noise is predominantly CONNECTED 2-D micro-structure with through-paths everywhere, not the closed pits and summits a prominence filter can see, while a 1-D transect crosses it at full amplitude. The deadband's amplitude filtering has no faithful raster analog via h-extrema; sigma-30 keeps the crown and the deployable default stands. Side-finding: a 30 m lattice point-sampled from the 5 m survey is itself a noisier chain than along-track sampling (c 5.91 vs 3.75 m/km) — regular-lattice aliasing is a grid-design cost." ;
  cito:citesAsEvidence :e75_prominence.csv ; dcterms:source :journal .
